<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE policyconfig PUBLIC
 "-//freedesktop//DTD PolicyKit Policy Configuration 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/PolicyKit/1/policyconfig.dtd">
<!--
  ===========================================================================
  org.aquariusos.gamemode.policy — switching between the desktop and Game Mode
  ===========================================================================
  PLAIN ENGLISH

  This file tells polkit — the part of Linux that decides who may do what —
  that AquariusOS has one new privileged job, and what to ask before doing it.

  The job is: tell the login screen which session to start next
  (/var/lib/AccountsService/users/<account>) and, for one switch, to log that
  person straight back in without a password (the TimedLogin lines in
  /etc/gdm/custom.conf). The program that does it is
  /usr/libexec/aquarius-session-root, and it does nothing else at all: it
  refuses any account that is not an ordinary person's account on this
  computer, and any session name that is not one of the session files this
  image actually ships.

  ⚠️ THE ANSWER BELOW IS "YES, WITH NO PASSWORD" (`yes`), WHICH IS UNUSUAL ON
  THIS COMPUTER, SO HERE IS THE WHOLE REASONING.

  Every other privileged job AquariusOS adds asks for an administrator password
  (auth_admin_keep). This one cannot, for a simple physical reason: the switch
  is meant to be pressed from the sofa with a game controller in your hands,
  and there is no way to type a password into a box with a controller. A
  password prompt here does not make the machine safer — it makes the feature
  not work, and the way people route around a feature that does not work is
  worse than the feature.

  WHAT IS ACTUALLY BEING RISKED, STATED PLAINLY. Somebody who can already run
  programs as the logged-in person, sitting at this computer, can change which
  session that same person's next login starts, and can make that next login
  happen without a password. They could already close the session, and they are
  already the person whose session it is. No file is read, no password is
  revealed, no other account is touched, and nothing here survives a restart
  with the boot setting at its default (aquarius-login-mode.service undoes the
  automatic login at every boot).

  WHO MAY ASK is narrowed hard in
  /usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/51-aquarius-game-mode.rules: the person at this
  screen, in the session that owns the screen right now, in the `wheel` group.
  Over SSH, from a switched-away session, or from a non-administrator account,
  the answer is a flat no.

  This is the same trade Valve, Bazzite and Nobara all make for the same
  button, on the same reasoning.
  ===========================================================================
-->
<policyconfig>

  <vendor>AquariusOS</vendor>
  <vendor_url>https://github.com/stoneharborent</vendor_url>

  <action id="org.aquariusos.gamemode.select">
    <description>Switch between the desktop and Game Mode</description>
    <message>Authentication is required to change which session this computer starts</message>
    <icon_name>input-gaming</icon_name>
    <defaults>
      <allow_any>no</allow_any>
      <allow_inactive>no</allow_inactive>
      <allow_active>auth_admin_keep</allow_active>
    </defaults>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.path">/usr/libexec/aquarius-session-root</annotate>
    <annotate key="org.freedesktop.policykit.exec.allow_gui">true</annotate>
  </action>

</policyconfig>
